/-!
# Clone certificates for clones too big to list

`Definability/CloneExtra4.lean` gives up at "four thousand operations is a fact about the board
that cannot be recorded as a proof", because `Magma.IsCloneFamily` checks the composition table
entry by entry and the table is `k²`. The index type is arbitrary, though, and the clones here have
a shape that removes the table altogether.

Close the clone of `Magma.freeCert50` and read its members as `16`-tuples. Six of the sixteen
entries are *free* -- they take all four values in all `4⁶ = 4096` combinations -- and each of the
other ten is a fixed unary map applied to one free entry, the four diagonal ones being constant
because the magma is idempotent. So a member is a choice of six digits,

    C d = fun x y ↦ P x y (d (K x y)),

composition of members is entrywise, hence entrywise on the digits, and every `P x y` is an
endomorphism of the magma. `IsCloneFamily.comp` is then a single application of that one fact,
proved by `decide` over `n⁴` cases instead of `k²` table entries. What remains exponential is the
refutation `∀ d, ¬ target`, which ranges over the `4096` operations and goes to `native_decide`.

Nothing here needs the family to *be* the clone. `IsCloneFamily` asks for closure under composition
and the two projections, and the clone is the smallest set with those properties, so a family that
passes them contains every binary term operation -- which is all the refutation needs. That is why
`Magma.freeCert61`, whose free entries range over subalgebras rather than over all of `Fin 6`, can
still be presented as `6⁵` operations indexed by five unrestricted digits.

These refute `TermDefinableFrom` and, the magmas being finite, its finite variant; they say nothing
about `DefinableFrom`.

This file is generated by `genfree.py`.
-/

